# Clean Claude Orphans

Reconciles installed Claude Code state (MCP servers, plugins, plugin marketplaces, skills)
against `{{ .chezmoi.sourceDir -}}/.chezmoidata/packages.yaml`, starting from
`audit-packages`'s findings. For every orphan, decide **REMOVE**, **KEEP**, or **DECLARE**
— never remove anything without confirming the full plan with the user first.

Use the `chezmoi` skill to add a DECLARE item back into `packages.yaml`.

This covers installed-but-undeclared drift only. For the mirror-image direction —
declared-but-locally-suppressed `skillOverrides`/`enabledPlugins` entries — run
`check-claude-overrides` instead (see `claude-tooling.md`'s "Checking for Override Drift"
section).

## Step 1: Gather orphans and environment context

```bash
~/.claude/skills/clean-claude-orphans/scripts/list-claude-orphans.sh
```

The script is a chezmoi template — this repo's root is baked into it at `chezmoi apply`
time (`dir .chezmoi.sourceDir`, since `.chezmoi.sourceDir` resolves to `home/` per
`.chezmoiroot`, not the repo root), so it needs no arguments.

Sources `scripts/audit-claude-code.sh` — the same shared library `audit-packages` itself
uses — and calls its functions directly to build the four Claude Code orphan sections,
plus the context needed before classifying anything. Every section is **JSON Lines** — one
JSON object per line, named fields instead of positional columns — so a row is
self-explanatory on its own, even pasted out of its `## section` without this file open
next to it:

- `## environments` — one object per `{path, current}`, from this machine's declared
  personas (`claude_envs` config), not a `~/.claude*` glob — avoids misreporting
  non-persona dirs that share the prefix (`.claude-code-session`, `.claude-monitor`,
  `.claude-worktrees`) as environments.
- `## {{ $localMarketplaceName }}-plugins` — one object per `{name, skills}` from this repo's own
  marketplace catalog (`{{ dir .chezmoi.sourceDir -}}/.claude-plugin/marketplace.json`);
  `skills` is `[]` when the plugin bundles none. Used to pre-tag skill orphans in
  `## orphans` — see Step 3.
- `## chezmoi-managed-skills` — one `{name}` object per skill directory under
  `{{ .chezmoi.sourceDir -}}/dot_claude/skills/`, deployed by `chezmoi apply` rather
  than `npx skills add`. Also used to pre-tag skill orphans in `## orphans`.
- `## installed-plugins` — one object per `{id, scope, projectPath, chezmoi_personal,
  enabled}`: `projectPath` is `null` outside project/local scope; `chezmoi_personal` is
  whether `id` ends `@{{ $localMarketplaceName }}` (never a DECLARE candidate — see Step 3). Needed
  before uninstalling anything, see Step 4. Sourced from `claude plugins list --json` (the
  same live CLI `audit-packages` itself reads), not the on-disk `installed_plugins.json`
  file, which can silently omit directory-marketplace-sourced plugins.
- `## orphans` — one object per `{category, item, skill_tag}` (`category` is
  `mcp_server`, `plugin`, `marketplace`, or `skill`; `skill_tag` is `bundled-by:<plugin>`
  for a skill duplicating a {{ $localMarketplaceName }} bundle, otherwise `null`). This section's job
  is to find what's **not chezmoi-managed** — not declared in packages.yaml, not listed in
  `## {{ $localMarketplaceName }}-plugins`, not found in `## chezmoi-managed-skills` — which is a
  different question from whether something is currently enabled. So two things never
  appear here at all, regardless of enabled state: a skill already listed in
  `## chezmoi-managed-skills`, and a plugin whose `id` ends `@{{ $localMarketplaceName }}` (declared
  by this repo's own marketplace manifest, a mechanism `packages.yaml` knows nothing
  about). `audit-packages` itself has no concept of either source, so without this
  filtering it would report both unconditionally, on every single run, independent of
  whether they're actually in use — being chezmoi-managed already answers the only
  question this section exists to answer. A `bundled-by:<plugin>` skill *duplicate* is
  different and stays in the list: the standalone copy itself genuinely isn't
  chezmoi-managed, even though a {{ $localMarketplaceName }} plugin covers the same functionality.

Read-only — changes nothing.

If the user already pasted `audit-packages` output, use that instead of re-running it, but
still run the script (or read the three files above directly) to get the marketplace,
chezmoi-managed-skills, and installed-plugins context it doesn't include.

## Step 2: Know the environment model before touching anything

- A machine may have several Claude Code environments (`~/.claude`, `~/.claude-work`,
  `~/.claude-personal`, ...) selected via `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`
  (`openspec/specs/claude-environments/spec.md`).
- **Skills are shared** across every environment via a symlink
  (`~/.claude-<name>/skills -> ~/.claude/skills`) — removing one removes it from all of them.
- **MCP servers and plugins are NOT shared** — each environment has independent state, and
  a `claude` invocation only sees the environment named by its own `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`. To
  check or clean a non-current environment, prefix every `claude` command with
  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<env_dir>` (e.g. `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude-work claude plugins list
  --json`), using the environment dirs Step 1 found. Don't declare a machine "clean" after
  only checking the current environment.
- Plugins also have an **enablement scope** separate from install scope: installed at user,
  project, or local scope, but a project can *enable* it independently via
  `.claude/settings.json` (`enabledPlugins`, tracked/shared) or `.claude/settings.local.json`
  (gitignored, personal). `packages.yaml` only records the user-scope install baseline — it
  says nothing about project/local enablement. Before uninstalling a plugin, check whether
  any project you know of still enables it; if so, uninstalling breaks that project's next
  session. Step 1's `## installed-plugins` section already tells you each plugin's scope,
  owning project, and whether it's currently enabled — see Step 4 for what to do with it.

## Step 3: Classify each orphan

Work marketplaces last — removing one can orphan the plugins installed from it.

### MCP servers
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Still declared in a project's own `.mcp.json` you use | **DECLARE** in packages.yaml |
| Duplicates a server a {{ $localMarketplaceName }} marketplace plugin already registers (`## {{ $localMarketplaceName }}-plugins` from Step 1 — e.g. `browser-tools`, `aws-local-dev`, `openspec-dashboard`) | **REMOVE** the manual entry; re-adding later is a one-line `enabledPlugins` toggle for that plugin, not a reinstall |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `claude mcp remove <name>` |

### Plugins

A `chezmoi_personal` plugin (`id` ends `@{{ $localMarketplaceName }}`) never appears in
`## orphans` — it's filtered out at the source (see Step 1), same reasoning as
chezmoi-managed skills. If one happens to be currently disabled, that's a separate
hygiene question from orphan detection — flag it in Step 3.5 if you notice it, don't
treat it as a REMOVE candidate here. Everything that *does* appear here needs one of:

| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| `enabled` is `true` for its row in `## installed-plugins` from Step 1 | **KEEP** (or **DECLARE** if it should be standard) |
| A {{ $localMarketplaceName }} marketplace plugin already covers the same functionality under a different name/marketplace (`## {{ $localMarketplaceName }}-plugins` from Step 1) | **REMOVE**, recommend switching to the {{ $localMarketplaceName }} one — re-adding is a one-line enable, not a fresh install |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `claude plugins uninstall <id>` (see Step 4 for scope handling) |

### Plugin marketplaces
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Still has any KEPT plugin installed from it (`claude plugins list --json`, `.id` ends `@<marketplace>`) | **KEEP** until those plugins are removed first |
| Name is a raw GitHub URL that duplicates an already-declared marketplace registered under a clean short name (e.g. an orphaned `https://github.com/org/foo.git` alongside a declared `foo`) | **REMOVE** — a shadow registration of the same source added by accident (e.g. `claude plugins marketplace add <url>` typed directly instead of enabling the existing one); check no installed plugin's `.id` ends `@<the-url>` before confirming |
| No remaining plugins | **REMOVE**: `claude plugins marketplace remove <name>` |

`{{ $localMarketplaceName }}` is declared in `packages.yaml`'s `plugin_marketplaces` like any other marketplace (registered at `~/.local/share/claude-plugins`, deployed there by `chezmoi apply` — see `openspec/specs/claude-plugin-marketplace/spec.md`), so it won't appear in `## orphans` at all — no special-case handling needed here.

### Skills

A chezmoi-managed skill (`## chezmoi-managed-skills` from Step 1) never appears in
`## orphans` — it's filtered out at the source, not merely tagged, since there's never a
REMOVE/DECLARE decision to make about it (see Step 1). Everything that *does* appear here
needs one of:

| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Referenced by name inside another skill or plugin you're keeping | **KEEP** — it's a dependency, not an orphan |
| `skill_tag` starts `bundled-by:` in `## orphans` from Step 1 (e.g. `bundled-by:code-quality`, `bundled-by:systems-design`) | **REMOVE** the standalone copy, keep the plugin-provided one — re-adding is a one-line plugin enable, not a reinstall |
| `audit-packages`'s wildcard-prefix match is a heuristic, not ground truth | Skim the skill's own `SKILL.md`/README before trusting a "covered by collection spec" note |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `rm -rf ~/.claude/skills/<name>` (affects every environment — Step 2) |

## Step 3.5: Unclutter — duplicates beyond the orphan list

The checks above also apply outside the orphan list: a plugin, skill, or MCP server can
duplicate something a {{ $localMarketplaceName }} plugin already provides while still being correctly
declared in packages.yaml (so `audit-packages` never flags it). Skim what's actually
installed — `claude plugins list --json`, `ls ~/.claude/skills/`, `claude mcp list` — against
Step 1's `## {{ $localMarketplaceName }}-plugins` catalog, not just the `## orphans` section.

The same `## installed-plugins` rows from Step 1 can also show the same plugin `id`
installed at **both** `user` scope and `project`/`local` scope (e.g. for `/Users/craig`
itself, not just a project repo — a project-scope entry can get created by running
`claude` directly from `$HOME`). The project/local entry adds nothing once the plugin is
already installed at user scope everywhere, so it's a decluttering candidate too — offer
to clear its `enabledPlugins` entry (Step 4's scope procedure) even though the plugin
itself is correctly declared and not an orphan.

A match is a decluttering candidate, not just an orphan: offer to **REMOVE** it and note
that re-adding it later is a one-line `enabledPlugins` toggle in whatever project needs it
(Step 2), not a packages.yaml edit or a fresh install. Fold these into the same
confirm-then-execute plan as Step 4 — don't run this as a separate pass.

## Step 4: Present the plan, confirm, then execute

List every orphan (and Step 3.5 candidate) with its verdict and a one-line reason. Ask the
user to confirm the whole plan, or adjust specific items, before running anything
destructive.

Within a category, remove plugins sourced from a marketplace before removing that
marketplace. For DECLARE items, edit `packages.yaml` (via the `chezmoi` skill) instead of
touching installed state.

### Removing a plugin: scope determines the procedure

Step 1's `## installed-plugins` section already tells you each plugin's `scope` and (for
project/local scope) `projectPath` — don't rediscover this by trial and error:

- **`user` scope**: `claude plugins uninstall <id>` works directly, from anywhere.
- **`project` or `local` scope**: `claude plugins uninstall <id>` fails until the
  `enabledPlugins` entry for it is removed from the owning project's `.claude/settings.json`
  (project scope, tracked with the team) or `.claude/settings.local.json` (local scope,
  gitignored) — the file lives at the `projectPath` Step 1 reported. Remove that entry, then
  re-run `claude plugins uninstall <id> --scope <project|local>` with your working directory
  inside `projectPath` — scope resolution is cwd-relative, so running it from elsewhere
  fails even with the right flag.

## Step 5: Verify

Re-run `audit-packages` (or the Step 1 script). Anything still listed should have an
explanation — most often a plugin still enabled by a project that wasn't checked.

## Common mistakes

- Uninstalling a project/local-scope plugin with a bare `claude plugins uninstall <id>` and
  no `--scope` flag, or from outside its owning `projectPath` — both fail. See Step 4.
- Removing the `{{ $localMarketplaceName }}` marketplace.
- Treating a skill orphan as machine-local — removing it removes it from every
  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` environment at once.
- Checking only the current `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` and calling the machine clean while
  sibling environments still carry orphans.
- Only scanning the `## orphans` list and missing Step 3.5 duplicates that are correctly
  declared but still redundant with a {{ $localMarketplaceName }} plugin.
